// Reading text back out of the flat store.
//
// `base` is the store address of source byte 0. str_of_mem(addr, n) copies n
// bytes into a fresh id string in one memcpy, so a token's spelling costs one
// allocation instead of one per character -- which is why the whole lexer is
// written against the store rather than against charat() on a string. See
// lex/README.md.

// The source bytes [a, b) as a string, cut short at the first NUL. b may run
// up to three bytes past the end of the input (the operator probes look two
// ahead); lex_mem's pad block keeps that in range, and a string cannot hold
// the NUL padding (docs/SPEC.md section 4), so the copy stops before it.
slice(word base, int a, int b) {
  int zl = buffer_length_before_zero(base + a, b - a);
  string s = str_of_mem(base + a, zl);
} return string s;
